insert into public.posts (id, campaign_id, theme, position, body, linkedin_url, status, scheduled_at, posted_at)
values ('abaa6dfe-32b7-425d-a6f0-37db73400f9f', '002dc3bc-983b-4153-9759-6fd8e6200887', 'In wellness tech, collecting less data isn’t a weakness, it’', 0, 'In wellness tech, collecting less data isn’t a weakness, it’s a sign of respect.

We don’t need to know everything about a person to help them feel better.
However, many wellness apps behave similarly.

The problem?
They collect every heartbeat, sleep cycle, step count and emotion, just in case. But “collect it now, figure out later” is not innovation, but it is laziness and it erodes trust.

How we do it differently at CipherCross ð

→  Minimum viable data, nothing more. We only collect what’s essential to deliver value. If a feature doesn’t need raw heart rate or sleep depth, we don’t store it.

→  Consent should be obvious, not hidden. Users choose exactly what to sync: step count, heart rate, mood, etc. All via simple toggle-based flows. No dark patterns. No forced sharing.

→  Local-first logic wherever possible. Sensitive calculations (like stress scoring or mood prediction) stay on the user’s device. We only receive aggregated results, never raw signals.

→  Encryption by default. Access tokens and health data are encrypted at rest; no exceptions.

Why does this matter?

In digital health, trust is the new currency.
And trust isn’t built by collecting more data, it’s built by collecting less, but using it better.

Users don’t owe us their biometric lives. They allow us to borrow just enough to help and return it with care. If wellness tech wants to stay human, privacy is a foundation.

Curious how this works in real-life products? Happy to share more behind the scenes.

